{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Final rebar-debug report\n\nProblem: the batched-map transient recovery test could recover its batched Git walk, then compare the exact SHA against None from a one-shot per-event oracle read.\n\nProven root cause: this was a test-oracle defect. Production resolve_event_commit deliberately maps every Git error, empty result, timeout, and exception to None (src/rebar/attest/authorship_resolution.py:8-65), and live authorship classification retains fail-closed behavior for an unresolved commit (src/rebar/_commands/verify_authorship.py:243-257). The fixture retried the broad walk but not the narrow per-event equivalence oracle.\n\nProof: in a disposable real Git repo, the batched map returned the exact event SHA; one injected per-event rc128 produced None on the first resolve_event_commit call, and an immediate identical second call returned the exact SHA. This ruled out topology, parser, pathspec, and attribution defects.\n\nTest: the final held-out oracle strengthened tests/unit/test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py:233-274 to inject one transient into both broad and narrow real git-log calls for rc128, rc0/empty, and BlockingIOError(EAGAIN). It was legitimately RED before repair (3 failed, exact SHA versus None), GREEN after restoration (4 focused tests passed), and an exact-defect mutation routing the assertion back to the one-shot resolver returned it to RED (3 failed). The persistent case at lines 298-319 proves exactly five attempts and fsck/count-objects diagnostics. The oracle asserts observable exact SHAs, so behavior-preserving refactors do not break it.\n\nFix: added the test-local _fixture_per_event_commit helper at lines 199-216, returning only an exact non-None SHA after at most five identical attempts and failing loudly with object-store forensics. Every direct batched/per-event equivalence assertion in the module now uses it, including the off-first-parent and ledger-parity cases. Production code is unchanged.\n\nValidation: target module 13 passed; adjacent authorship/identity/compaction selection 38 passed; make lint PASS; make typecheck PASS; git diff --check PASS. The final formatter-only adjustment was followed by another target-module run: 13 passed.\n\nScope and safety: only tests/unit/test_authorship_batched_ticket_map_7084.py changed. The repair strengthens the existing exact equivalence oracle, creates no production capability, adds no dependency, removes no exact assertion, and preserves the intentional production fail-closed contract.\n\nSiblings: construct sweep found no remaining direct resolve_event_commit equivalence assertions in this module; all route through the bounded helper. Production callers were intentionally excluded because they depend on fail-closed None semantics. Parity census is N/A: this is a test-local oracle seam, not a CLI/MCP/library/automation or dual-direction product operation.\n\nResidual risk and prevention: accepted—the production resolver cannot distinguish transient rc0/empty from a genuine no-match, and one narrow walk is documented at 6.78s on a 71k-commit history (authorship_resolution.py:21-30); production retry would multiply genuine no-match cost, so recovery remains fixture-only and bounded. No other observed fragility remains undispositioned.\n\nReplication: the original one-shot broad+narrow transient scenario now passes for all three failure modes against unchanged real fixture history."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787027212064450001,"uuid":"5bddf3e5-6cc5-4f1e-9780-7c9fdd3a1a6a"}